WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.668
[MUSIC].
[APPLAUSE].

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:17.171
Prime Minister Harper, thank you so
much for joining us today.

00:00:17.171 --> 00:00:19.631
&gt;&gt; Well, thanks for having me.

00:00:19.631 --> 00:00:20.795
&gt;&gt; Before we begin,

00:00:20.795 --> 00:00:25.931
could I please get a quick show of hands
from all the Canadians in the audience?

00:00:25.931 --> 00:00:27.130
Raise your hands?

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.451
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:00:28.451 --> 00:00:33.400
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; An all Canadian campus here,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.110
who would have known?

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.775
&gt;&gt; You've clearly got some pull
around here, Mr. Prime Minister.

00:00:37.775 --> 00:00:39.402
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:00:39.402 --> 00:00:42.430
&gt;&gt; We have many topics to discuss today,

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:46.780
from your views on key policy issues to
moments of leadership on the world stage.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.690
Before we start though, I wanted to
start with a very simple question.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.710
Is it true that, despite writing
a book about Canadian hockey,

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.120
you don't have a favorite hockey team?

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.155
&gt;&gt; No, that's not true.

00:01:00.155 --> 00:01:05.170
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I never was very clear in

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:10.180
public as to what my favorite team was
because I always felt that I could lose

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.979
more votes on answering that question-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:12.979 --> 00:01:15.740
&gt;&gt; Than any serious issue of policy.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:19.810
We actually have two favorite teams in
our household, the Toronto Maple Leafs.

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:23.680
I grew up in Toronto,
my father was a Maple Leafs fan.

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.570
And then of course, as an adult, I've
resided most of my adult life in Calgary.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.530
So we have the Toronto Maple Leafs in the
east and the Calgary Flames in the west,

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.110
but since Canada hasn't won
the Stanley Cup since 1993,

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:35.235
I will take any one of them.

00:01:35.235 --> 00:01:36.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Any one of them,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:37.915
I'm desperate at this point.

00:01:37.915 --> 00:01:40.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Clearly judging by this year's

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:44.870
performances at the Olympics, there is
a lot to admire about Canadian sport.

00:01:44.870 --> 00:01:47.940
So I'll take that.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:51.730
Now I wanted to shift to your
beginnings as a politician,

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.690
back when you were still in university.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:56.790
What originally drew you to politics?

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.550
&gt;&gt; Well it's a long question,
it's a long answer.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:06.440
First of all, I never in my university
days, I never intended to be a politician,

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:12.050
that was not the plan, to the extent
I had a plan that was not the plan.

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:17.100
I was interested in politics because
as a very, it started as a kid.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:22.010
From the time I was about nine years old,
my parents expected me to read

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:27.240
the front page of the newspaper and
to be conversant in public issues

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.164
at the dinner table, so
I kind of started that interest early.

00:02:31.164 --> 00:02:35.720
In university, I was studying economics,
I became very interested in public policy,

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:41.760
and my interest, then, and my first
job in politics was as a researcher.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.470
And that was kind of how I saw myself,
was essentially a research assistant,

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:49.380
and I had longer term plans
to become an economist and

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:52.310
to be involved in public policy and
academia.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:54.000
So that was really the beginning of it,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.279
and it's a lot of steps
that led me to where I was.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.330
&gt;&gt; And
I'd love to get to some of those steps.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.900
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; We talk a lot about entrepreneurship

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:04.580
here at the GSB.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:09.255
And your political career shows many
signs of being very entrepreneurial,

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:14.224
especially when you united the different
right-leaning parties within Canada

00:03:14.224 --> 00:03:16.140
under one banner in 2003.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.269
Could you talk a little bit about how you
managed to pull off that difficult task,

00:03:20.269 --> 00:03:22.590
and why you were the right person for
the job?

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:25.830
&gt;&gt; But let me maybe just go
through how I ended up in the job,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:29.650
because I have the most unusual
path to the Prime Ministership of

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:34.180
anyone in Canadian history, the only
one to come up through a third party.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:40.140
So, I went to Ottawa in 1985
as a research assistant,

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:45.820
an economics graduate, to assist
the government on its program of deficit

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:49.980
reduction, and market reforms, and some
of the other things that we believed in.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.080
Left that job after a year,

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.480
I'd only intended to be there two years
maximum, but left after one year,

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:01.180
fairly disillusioned with what the
government was not doing on these matters.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:03.530
I was convinced that we'd
never balance a budget,

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:05.930
in fact, that turned out to be the case.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:08.870
So I actually not only left the job,
I actually left the party,

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:11.640
went back to university
to get a graduate degree.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:15.780
And in the course of the graduate degree,
people heard me talking about my

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:20.730
experiences, and a number of professors,
who had similar thinking hooked me up

00:04:20.730 --> 00:04:24.760
with a fellow named Preston Manning,
who was forming a new political party to

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:29.180
push some of these issues much harder,
eventually called the Reform Party.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:34.420
So I got involved in that in 1987 I became
the first policy chief of the party.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:38.360
I did it, this is actually true,
I remember say to the then-president,

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.890
who became eventually
a colleague in parliament,

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:45.100
a ministerial colleague, I remember saying
to her, she asked my why I was doing this.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:49.100
I said, well, I sort of like what they're
doing here, and this is a fun hobby,

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:50.475
it's not going to take much of my time.

00:04:50.475 --> 00:04:54.580
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Anyway, one thing led to another, and

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:58.670
the first time we ran for election,
the election was suddenly upon us,

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:02.160
we had almost no candidates,
so I became a candidate.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.540
Obviously didn't win, we didn't win any
seats, but I did surprisingly well.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.700
And then the next time around, I decided
to run again when we had a much better

00:05:11.700 --> 00:05:14.150
shot, and I ran and got elected in 1993.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:19.800
By the end of my first term,
look, I say there’s no plan here.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:25.930
By the end of my first term,
I could also see that the reform party,

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:30.770
as I had envisioned it,
was kind of stalled, and

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:34.780
once again, I decided it was time for
me to do something different, I went and

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:39.050
headed a essentially conservative
political action committee in Canada.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:41.970
So I left, I did not run for re-election.

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:46.580
And I did that for three or four years,
and I was about to depart that job into

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:50.890
a true private sector job, when the party
that I'd left, the Reform Party,

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:54.670
which had converted itself into something
called the Reform Conservative Alliance,

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:58.950
when it had a massive civil war
in the party around leadership.

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:03.050
And it was so bad that the party sunk
to the low single digits in support,

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.360
was almost ready to drop off the map, and

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:10.660
virtually everybody of some note in the
party was discredited by the civil war.

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:15.240
And since I was not there,
I was the only person of

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.500
any stature in the party who wasn't
tainted by the civil war, and

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:21.750
a lot of the MPs approached me,
would you come and lead us?

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:25.310
And we just paid off
the mortgage on our home.

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:28.080
And I said to my wife, if I do this?

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.390
It wasn't what I was thinking of, if I
do this, I'm going to become leader of

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:35.320
the opposition without trying,
like I will win this race, if I get in.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:38.750
So I got in, she agreed,
I got in, I won the race.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:45.950
And then the other conservative
party had a massive civil war.

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:50.360
And it wasn't going well,
we were all looking at oblivion.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:55.760
And one thing led to another, both our
parties were ready to do a merger,

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.790
we both understood that
if we stayed separate,

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:02.260
our chances of, not just winning,
our chances of survival were limited.

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:07.460
So people really did it, in some ways out
of desperation, the timing was just right.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.870
Partly because my background was
a mixed partisan background,

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:14.290
I was kind of a good person
to lead that effort,

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:17.280
had a good partner on the other
side named Peter McKay.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:21.050
And we united the parties, and then
the next thing that happened was the then

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:24.460
governing liberal party was so certain
that it couldn't possibly lose an election

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:27.060
that it engaged in it's own civil war.

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:30.780
And I ended up leading in the next two
elections the party that was united, and

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:34.209
they led the party that was divided, and
in politics the guy united usually wins.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:37.690
&gt;&gt; It sounds like it pays to
be the last man standing.

00:07:37.690 --> 00:07:39.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:43.020
I do have an interesting story on that,
it's funny.

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:48.360
After the last election,
I had two or three colleagues,

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.020
I'd stayed in Parliament a while, and
some former colleagues visited me.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:56.440
And I did point out to them, I remember
saying, first time it surprised me, but

00:07:56.440 --> 00:07:57.230
I tried it again.

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:02.940
I said, I am the only member of parliament
from the great revolution of 1993.

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:08.637
The only one of the class of 93 still in
parliament, and I'm the last man standing.

00:08:08.637 --> 00:08:10.530
Who would have believed that?

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:14.330
And everyone of them said to me, we
always thought that was going to happen.

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:16.472
So they saw it before I did.

00:08:16.472 --> 00:08:19.955
&gt;&gt; Well, it's good that to be
a rock in the storm if you will.

00:08:19.955 --> 00:08:21.679
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:08:21.679 --> 00:08:25.588
&gt;&gt; You were also known within Canada for
positioning yourself

00:08:25.588 --> 00:08:30.210
as a progressive conservative when
you ran for election in 2006.

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:33.050
What I wanted to ask you next was,

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:39.039
how did you decide which sacrifices
you would make in order to govern,

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:43.530
and which principles you
would not compromise?

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:47.650
&gt;&gt; Yeah look, I would not have described
myself as a progressive conservative.

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:51.600
Part of the issue in Canada is that is the
name of one of the two founding parties.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.739
So that would have identified you
with one party rather than the other.

00:08:56.430 --> 00:08:59.980
Look, I consider myself
a straight conservative.

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.170
I'm conservative on most issues.

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:08.970
I'm conservative probably more in the
temperamental than the ideological sense.

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:15.600
And I'm a strong believer, I get this
is where people consider it moderate.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:19.790
I'm a strong believer In
incrementalism in policy.

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:24.162
And I think some describe my government
as kind of a series of what they call

00:09:24.162 --> 00:09:29.044
this incrementalism in terms of the steps
we took across ranges of public policy.

00:09:29.044 --> 00:09:34.136
Partly because, and not that sometimes
you have to do radical departures, but

00:09:34.136 --> 00:09:39.380
generally speaking I'm distrustful of
frankly, smart people like ourselves,

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:44.860
with great blueprints that we're going to
kind of invent the solutions to the world.

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:47.370
I actually think you have
to see how things work,

00:09:47.370 --> 00:09:49.230
what unintended consequences are,

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:53.650
whether something is turning out as you
expect before you move to the next step.

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:56.670
So I tend to be someone that
wants to gather data and

00:09:56.670 --> 00:10:00.070
see what the experience is
until I take the next step.

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:04.240
&gt;&gt; So when it comes to gathering data,
incrementalism, and

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:09.857
dealing with very weighty policy issues,
one that's very relevant today and

00:10:09.857 --> 00:10:13.535
where Canada gets a lot of
acclaim is immigration.

00:10:13.535 --> 00:10:18.394
And President Trump has cited Canada as
an example that the US should potentially

00:10:18.394 --> 00:10:23.590
follow, and you've been known to
support skilled immigration to Canada.

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:25.780
What lessons, incremental or

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:30.460
otherwise, could US policy makers
learn from Canada's example?

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:31.910
&gt;&gt; Well, look, I'd say two things, and

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:37.260
I'd say this not to just American
audiences, but audiences everywhere else.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:41.160
What I think was most remarkable for

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:43.990
other political leaders about
our immigration policy was not,

00:10:43.990 --> 00:10:48.960
for the most part they were successful
policies in the policy sense.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:53.610
It's that we had overwhelming public
support behind our immigration policy,

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:56.030
behind a large scale immigration policy.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:58.410
How do you do that?

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:06.100
First of all, on the success side, we were
progressively moving our system towards

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:10.435
being based on labor market needs,
on responding to labor market needs.

00:11:10.435 --> 00:11:16.260
There's always been a significant portion
of the Canadian, unlike the United States,

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:20.700
always a significant portion of Canadian
immigration policy that was admitted

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.580
on the basis of skills and
labor force considerations.

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:31.430
We vastly increased that over
time from 40% to two-thirds.

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:36.780
We started by making, it was really
a simple change, but it worked wonders was

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:43.240
allowing university students to find work
in their areas while they were in Canada,

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:46.810
which would often encourage those
students to stay once they graduated.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:50.750
A really simple change made
a big difference in policy.

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:53.840
So I'm a strong, strong believer that

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:58.610
immigration policy in the modern age
must be based to be beneficial for

00:11:58.610 --> 00:12:02.720
an economy, must be based on labor
force needs and requirements.

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:06.200
Doesn't mean you can't have humanitarian
and family reunification streams, but

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:10.710
that is not in my mind the central
benefit of an immigration policy for

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:12.190
one's own country.

00:12:12.190 --> 00:12:15.212
The second thing, and let me say this
because I'm in the United States.

00:12:15.212 --> 00:12:18.846
I'm often asked in the United States or
Europe,

00:12:18.846 --> 00:12:23.470
how can we make our current
immigration system more popular?

00:12:23.470 --> 00:12:24.610
Why is it so unpopular?

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:26.670
Well, I'll tell you this right now.

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:30.690
If Canada had a huge percentage of as
you do in the United States or Europe,

00:12:30.690 --> 00:12:34.450
a huge percentage of its immigrant
population was here illegally or

00:12:34.450 --> 00:12:38.400
irregularly outside the law,
it would not be popular.

00:12:38.400 --> 00:12:42.970
Illegal immigration is not popular
anywhere in the world at any time.

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:47.240
And if you want to sustain public support
for an immigration system, this has to

00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:51.690
be a decision the society takes
through its laws and enforces the law.

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:53.640
And unless you can fix that problem, and

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.170
I think that is the big
problem in the United States.

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:56.715
Without a solution to that problem,

00:12:56.715 --> 00:13:01.798
people's resistance to legal
immigration has gone through the roof.

00:13:01.798 --> 00:13:06.580
And my quick comment on the United States,

00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:10.700
the big problem you have is it's way too
difficult for someone to become a legal

00:13:10.700 --> 00:13:14.160
immigrant, and way too easy for
them to become an illegal immigrant.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:18.010
&gt;&gt; There seems to be also a flip side to
what you're saying when it comes to public

00:13:18.010 --> 00:13:18.850
support.

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:23.810
Which is in addition to upholding the law,
there is this embrace of multiculturalism

00:13:23.810 --> 00:13:28.130
in Canada that seems very unique and
distinctly Canadian.

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:32.978
And so I what I wanted to follow
up with is in an age of rising

00:13:32.978 --> 00:13:37.523
nationalism in Canada,
has this unique position and

00:13:37.523 --> 00:13:42.090
it seems to be both multi-cultural and
patriotic.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:46.540
I'm curious, do you think this is
a blueprint for other nations to follow,

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:50.980
that you can be both things, and
if yes, how can we encourage both?

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:52.850
&gt;&gt; Yeah, look, I think absolutely.

00:13:52.850 --> 00:13:57.820
First of all, I think Canada had a bit of
a traditional advantage on that from other

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:06.490
countries in that Canada has never had
one clearly defined national culture.

00:14:06.490 --> 00:14:10.070
And we've always had at least, we've had
two national languages, we've always had,

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:14.630
at least, going back two major religions,
and obviously it's more diverse today.

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:19.960
So there was never a kind of a unified
vision of what a Canadian looked like,

00:14:19.960 --> 00:14:22.180
so I think that background kind of helped.

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:28.830
But on multiculturalism,
we actually modified the multiculturalism

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:33.520
policy somewhat along the lines really
of how it's practiced in Quebec.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:37.520
And technically in Quebec, they don't
call it multiculturalism, they call

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:41.040
it [FOREIGN], which interculturalism,
doesn't mean anything in English.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:48.020
But what it really is,
is it is about encouraging,

00:14:48.020 --> 00:14:53.760
I would say promoting is not the word,
but encouraging immigrants to retain

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:58.520
aspects of their identity, their links
to their home country, their traditions.

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:03.720
But encouraging that within the concept
of a broader social unity and

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:06.100
a broader participation.

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:12.620
And we as a government really saw
multiculturalism, well it had obviously

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:18.310
the aspect of preserving people's
native and a diverse set of cultures.

00:15:18.310 --> 00:15:22.070
Really, its objective was
ultimately integration.

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:25.711
It stems from the belief, and I do believe
this strongly, that first and foremost,

00:15:25.711 --> 00:15:29.170
immigrants, when they come to a new
country, they want to belong.

00:15:29.170 --> 00:15:30.800
They actually don't
want to be ghetto-ized.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:34.330
They may want to retain aspects of
their personal and family identities,

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:36.190
but they want to belong.

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:39.640
And so we encourage that,
but at the same time,

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:44.140
we recognize that as you diversify the
ethnic and religious mix of your country,

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:48.740
that inevitably that will have some impact
on the mainstream of your own culture.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:53.490
So that was really the philosophy behind
the policy, and we think it's, look,

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:55.290
I think that it's objectively successful.

00:15:55.290 --> 00:16:00.940
Canada has few Kind of ethnic based,
deeply ethnic based conflicts.

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:06.580
It's a very diverse society where
people integrate reasonably well and

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:09.160
this is going back decades.

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:13.250
And from my standpoint,
the Conservative Party of Canada

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:18.030
is one of the few center-right parties in
the world that wins a large share, and

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:22.000
in the case of 2011,
an outright majority of immigrant vote.

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:26.340
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a powerful set of options.

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:29.120
Future policymakers, please take note.

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:33.340
I'd love to build on
this notion of belonging,

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:36.010
but now on the international stage.

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:38.510
You're a passionate free trader.

00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:43.730
Under your watch, Canada signed landmark
free trade deals with South Korea and

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:47.780
the European Union, and you're known
to be a supporter of NAFTA as well.

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:51.230
What are the best ways
to promote the benefits

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:56.100
of free trade in an increasingly
anti-trade global political environment?

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:58.950
&gt;&gt; Sure, you might be a bit
surprised by my answer on this.

00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:03.730
I'm actually writing a book on this
very subject, on the rise of populism,

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:07.780
what's driving it, and particularly
how conservatives should respond.

00:17:07.780 --> 00:17:09.179
First of all, just on the record,

00:17:09.179 --> 00:17:12.091
when we came to office,
when my government came to office in 2006.

00:17:12.091 --> 00:17:16.605
In spite of the fact that Canada is one
of the most open economies in the world,

00:17:16.605 --> 00:17:20.520
we had free trade agreements
with only five countries.

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:23.230
And when I left we had concluded
negotiations with 51 and

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:28.300
now all are in the process, are either
implemented or being implemented.

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:32.760
And by the way, that was another thing, in
an era where there's increasing resistance

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:37.800
to trade, that was another thing we
did with overwhelming public support.

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:42.340
None of those trade agreements
were unpopular, so why is that?

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:47.630
And what I tell policy makers
particularly the conservative

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:52.700
policy makers who form
the International Democrat Union.

00:17:52.700 --> 00:17:57.473
What I tell them is it is not a simple
matter of being for free trade.

00:17:57.473 --> 00:18:02.460
I understand David Ricardo, I understand
the basic theories of free trade,

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:05.160
but that's not good enough.

00:18:05.160 --> 00:18:09.370
A trade deal is a large commercial deal,
and this is actually, to some degree,

00:18:09.370 --> 00:18:11.500
where I agree with what
President Trump is saying.

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:15.630
You actually have to know what you're
doing when you sign such a deal.

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:17.930
You can't do it on the basis of theory, or

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:23.720
the basis of simply bureaucratic,
internal consultations and negotiations.

00:18:23.720 --> 00:18:29.270
When we did trade deals we
established comprehensive

00:18:29.270 --> 00:18:34.390
consultations with every single
major sector of Canadian society.

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:37.930
We understood at the bargaining table,

00:18:37.930 --> 00:18:42.240
what the interests of our
economy actually were.

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:46.360
We understood both what interest we
needed to advance in terms of trade,

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:49.530
we also understood what
interest we needed to protect.

00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:53.350
Because protection is part of your
responsibility as a government when

00:18:53.350 --> 00:18:54.510
those are your interest.

00:18:54.510 --> 00:18:56.140
So we understood what the interest are,

00:18:56.140 --> 00:18:58.710
obviously we would lean
to opening up markets.

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:02.190
And we ultimately understood what kind of
concessions we could and could not make,

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:05.070
where we could make gains, and
where we could not make gains.

00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:07.630
And particularly,
the additional factor we had

00:19:07.630 --> 00:19:10.940
in place was when we dealt with larger
entities like the European Union.

00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:15.515
We had to know what they wanted, and
we spent a lot of time finding that out.

00:19:15.515 --> 00:19:20.340
So, it comes down to treating
it as a serious commercial deal.

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:23.720
Understanding all of the periods and
commas and

00:19:23.720 --> 00:19:25.990
making a good deal for the country.

00:19:25.990 --> 00:19:28.130
And is it possible,

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:33.260
President Trump doesn't say he's
a protectionist, maybe he is.

00:19:33.260 --> 00:19:35.240
He says he's for
good deals and not bad deals.

00:19:35.240 --> 00:19:37.140
Is it possible to have the bad trade deal?

00:19:37.140 --> 00:19:41.032
Absolutely, and there are many times I
would've walked away from the table rather

00:19:41.032 --> 00:19:42.320
than signed what was on it.

00:19:43.690 --> 00:19:48.810
&gt;&gt; There's something else that you said
about overwhelming public support though

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:51.465
and essentially selling these deals or

00:19:51.465 --> 00:19:56.620
cutting these deals to last within
the court of public opinions.

00:19:56.620 --> 00:20:01.120
So, is there a component to this to
winning hearts and not just minds?

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:05.050
&gt;&gt; Yeah, look I think advantage
we start with in Canada

00:20:06.250 --> 00:20:11.450
was we had, our first free trade
deal besides confederation itself.

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:16.630
Our first free trade deal was
the Canada-US Agreement concluded in 1987.

00:20:16.630 --> 00:20:21.470
It became the focal point of
the 1988 federal election.

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:26.710
In which the then progressive conservative
government was championing the deal.

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:30.685
And the opposition, the liberals and the
new Democratic party, were opposing it.

00:20:30.685 --> 00:20:36.970
And the rhetoric got very high
according to the opposition,

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:41.560
signing of the free trade deal would
devastate multiple Canadian industries.

00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:44.470
Basically hollow out the Canadian
economy and eventually

00:20:44.470 --> 00:20:48.330
we'd lose our independence and become
the 51st state of the United States.

00:20:48.330 --> 00:20:51.610
This was literally,
in fact a famous ad from that campaign,

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:54.000
almost no one here is
old enough to know this.

00:20:54.000 --> 00:20:59.410
Famous ad from that campaign was two
negotiators sitting at a table and

00:20:59.410 --> 00:21:03.510
the American says, let's just,

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:07.310
I think we've got a deal if we just
kind of take out this one line.

00:21:07.310 --> 00:21:09.035
And you go back and
the line was the border.

00:21:09.035 --> 00:21:13.120
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Between Canada and the United States.

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:16.370
But the government won the election,

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:18.650
principly on a split
vote of the opposition.

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:21.760
They won the election and
they passed the deal.

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:26.830
And not only did none of the things
the opposition predicted come true, but

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:31.040
frankly the deal, in its economic
performance and the eventual NAFTA,

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:35.600
exceeded all forecasts in terms
of its encouragement of trade and

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:36.850
economic growth.

00:21:36.850 --> 00:21:40.170
So you have that backdrop that
Canada is a trading country.

00:21:40.170 --> 00:21:45.200
And with that experience no one really
believes kind of hysteria about trade but

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:46.260
look, I will tell you this.

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:48.120
We didn’t operate on that alone,

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:51.900
I talked about the kind of
comprehensive consultations we ran.

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:54.750
And I can tell you this, when I got
in front of the Canadian public,

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:57.150
whether it was in Brussels or

00:21:57.150 --> 00:22:03.310
somewhere in Canada to TPP in the case
to announce a major trade agreement.

00:22:03.310 --> 00:22:07.950
When I got in front of people and
then every group in the country,

00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:11.300
every interest group, every sector would
put out its release saying they were for

00:22:11.300 --> 00:22:14.430
the deal, against the deal or
mixed feelings.

00:22:14.430 --> 00:22:17.240
I knew in advance what every

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:20.000
single interest group in
the country group was going to say.

00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:22.600
We were not guessing about public opinion.

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:26.010
We knew exactly, so yeah,
you win hearts and minds.

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:29.584
But actually, this is not an issue.

00:22:29.584 --> 00:22:32.740
These are people's vested
economic interests.

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:37.110
You cannot treat them as
a strictly a philosophical battle.

00:22:37.110 --> 00:22:40.970
It's about really understanding
where the rubber meets the road and

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:43.187
where people will see the gain or lose.

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:48.300
&gt;&gt; When it comes to interests, I'd love
to talk a little bit now about foreign

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:52.900
policy interests, and specifically
one example of your leadership.

00:22:54.320 --> 00:22:59.990
I'll lay out the scenario first,
it's November of 2014, you're at

00:22:59.990 --> 00:23:05.870
the G20 Summit in Australia, and Vladimir
Putin comes up to you to shake your hand.

00:23:05.870 --> 00:23:09.680
You accept the handshake, reluctantly and

00:23:09.680 --> 00:23:13.610
you say to him,
you need to get out of Ukraine.

00:23:13.610 --> 00:23:16.040
Take us what went through
your head at that moment.

00:23:16.040 --> 00:23:20.970
&gt;&gt; Sure, well,
I'll tell you more of the story.

00:23:23.150 --> 00:23:25.620
First of all, like so many leaders,

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:31.290
I had given Vladimir Putin the benefit of
the doubt when I first came to office.

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:34.930
He had come to power in Russia
after a terrible decade,

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:37.000
where the company literally fell apart.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:41.490
And the economic and social indicators for
Russia from that period are some of

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:44.850
the worst,
it wasn't just the political collapse.

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:49.120
Some of the worst social and economic
collapse any major country's ever seen.

00:23:49.120 --> 00:23:54.380
And so he came to power, reestablished
order, seemed to be a strong leader.

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:57.132
So people gave him the benefit
of the doubt at first.

00:23:57.132 --> 00:24:00.248
I gave him a benefit there
a couple of years and

00:24:00.248 --> 00:24:03.775
it increasingly was obvious
to me that he was not and

00:24:03.775 --> 00:24:08.139
never going to be a friend, and
we can talk about that if you want.

00:24:08.139 --> 00:24:09.111
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:24:09.111 --> 00:24:13.266
frankly, I became in private meetings,
increasingly blunt with Mr.

00:24:13.266 --> 00:24:16.323
Putin unlike some of my
international colleagues.

00:24:16.323 --> 00:24:19.430
In fact,
I think I became kind of the bad cop.

00:24:19.430 --> 00:24:22.150
Bad cop,
good cop at Summer International Forums.

00:24:22.150 --> 00:24:25.460
So Vladimir Putin was
not unaware of my views.

00:24:25.460 --> 00:24:29.173
And then of course, and
I had been actually even a year or

00:24:29.173 --> 00:24:31.549
two before the invasion of Crimea.

00:24:31.549 --> 00:24:35.910
I had been advocating for
his removal from the G8, not very subtly.

00:24:35.910 --> 00:24:39.500
Certainly not subtly in private,
and not even that subtly in public.

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:45.402
And so he invades Crimea and
Eastern Ukraine,

00:24:45.402 --> 00:24:50.849
and it was really my
first meeting with Mr.

00:24:50.849 --> 00:24:54.193
Putin since that period.

00:24:54.193 --> 00:24:55.750
Let me just tell you
a bit of the backdrop.

00:24:55.750 --> 00:24:58.080
What happens at international conferences?

00:24:58.080 --> 00:24:59.384
You see these things,

00:24:59.384 --> 00:25:04.196
you would not believe how much time you
spend arriving at an international summit.

00:25:04.196 --> 00:25:08.155
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; There is an order in which you arrive.

00:25:08.155 --> 00:25:12.705
It's all established by protocol and you
all kind of drive up one at a time, and

00:25:12.705 --> 00:25:16.705
you assemble in a room behind, before
the conference begins, you come out and

00:25:16.705 --> 00:25:18.505
arrive again on the stage.

00:25:18.505 --> 00:25:20.505
It takes like two or
three hours to arrive.

00:25:22.595 --> 00:25:27.365
So anyway, I'd gone through and I was,
they were doing the backwards order,

00:25:27.365 --> 00:25:30.430
the most junior to the most senior,
and prime ministers,

00:25:30.430 --> 00:25:34.490
no matter how long you're in office, are
always considered junior to presidents.

00:25:34.490 --> 00:25:38.930
So, I had already arrived in the room
backstage with most of the leaders.

00:25:38.930 --> 00:25:42.150
Putin would have been near
the end of the arrivals, and

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:45.490
it was interesting because,
I tell this story for a bunch of reasons.

00:25:46.690 --> 00:25:47.600
So I'm in the room.

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:48.472
I'm at one end.

00:25:48.472 --> 00:25:51.995
Mr. Putin comes in and
everybody's greeting him and

00:25:51.995 --> 00:25:56.860
slapping him on the back, and all
the things we do at these things that we-

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:57.880
&gt;&gt; High fives.

00:25:57.880 --> 00:25:59.810
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we know all these people.

00:25:59.810 --> 00:26:03.119
You get quite comfortable
with these people.

00:26:03.119 --> 00:26:04.090
You get to know them after time.

00:26:04.090 --> 00:26:06.549
Well, anyway, Putin comes around to me.

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:12.480
And he sticks out his hand, and I had
kind of thought what am I going to do or

00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:16.310
say if Putin, candidate support for
Ukraine was extremely strong.

00:26:16.310 --> 00:26:19.210
Obviously, I denounced them privately.

00:26:19.210 --> 00:26:22.334
And so I said to him, I shook his hand.

00:26:22.334 --> 00:26:26.627
I said I'll shake your hand, Vladimir, but
I have only one thing to tell you, and

00:26:26.627 --> 00:26:28.069
that is get out of Ukraine.

00:26:29.370 --> 00:26:34.610
And he paused,
looked at me kind of taken aback and

00:26:34.610 --> 00:26:37.335
said to me, I'm not in Ukraine.

00:26:37.335 --> 00:26:43.000
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; To which I said,

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.409
well, that's why it's a waste
of my time to talk to you.

00:26:45.409 --> 00:26:48.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And by the way, I was just reading,

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:50.050
Teddy Roosevelt said and this is so true.

00:26:50.050 --> 00:26:53.247
Teddy Roosevelt said about
the Russians in 1905.

00:26:53.247 --> 00:26:56.194
He said, the most annoying
things about the Russians,

00:26:56.194 --> 00:26:59.970
is they will lie to you even when they
know that you know they're lying.

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:03.085
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's an inexplicable national trait.

00:27:03.085 --> 00:27:10.450
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But, anyway, so I said that to Putin.

00:27:10.450 --> 00:27:13.300
And the reason I tell this story,
is what's interesting after that,

00:27:14.660 --> 00:27:18.340
the story got out, by the way, because
the Russians put the story out, not me.

00:27:18.340 --> 00:27:19.850
I don't know why.

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:23.460
But what's interesting is all the other
leaders witnessed this exchange.

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:26.410
And what went through their heads,

00:27:26.410 --> 00:27:29.650
obviously the ones who were facing the
electorates where Putin and his actions

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:34.210
were not popular, all of a sudden,
they ceased being friendly with him.

00:27:34.210 --> 00:27:35.610
Because they knew if the story got out,

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:39.900
while Harper was not friendly with
them and we were, that would be bad.

00:27:39.900 --> 00:27:42.300
So nobody was friendly with them for
the rest of the conference.

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:45.875
And then there were pictures of them
sitting at tables eating by himself, and

00:27:45.875 --> 00:27:46.817
that sort of thing.

00:27:46.817 --> 00:27:47.973
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But look,

00:27:47.973 --> 00:27:53.555
I point that out only because, and it's
not that symbolic actions are everything,

00:27:53.555 --> 00:27:58.610
but it is interesting how sometimes
simply by taking a stand.

00:27:58.610 --> 00:28:02.429
You can people to act.

00:28:02.429 --> 00:28:05.773
&gt;&gt; And it seems that this time now,
especially knowing what we know,

00:28:05.773 --> 00:28:08.840
that that was quite courageous and
prescient.

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:11.655
&gt;&gt; Well, I don't go to rush on business,
let's put it that way, so.

00:28:11.655 --> 00:28:15.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; How would you handle Putin

00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:18.260
in today's world, almost four years later?

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:22.490
&gt;&gt; Well, it's the same thing.

00:28:22.490 --> 00:28:27.450
Look, there's no way of avoiding the fact
that Russia is a major power and

00:28:27.450 --> 00:28:28.930
cannot be ignored.

00:28:28.930 --> 00:28:32.210
We brought in a host of sanctions
on Russia in coordination with

00:28:32.210 --> 00:28:33.830
the Obama administration and others.

00:28:33.830 --> 00:28:36.270
We would keep those sanctions in place.

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:40.650
And I would continue to minimize
contact with Putin, except where it's

00:28:40.650 --> 00:28:46.130
unavoidable or necessary to conduct
foreign policy as in Syria, for example.

00:28:46.130 --> 00:28:51.130
But I would not pretend
I'm friends with him and

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:55.240
it's my conviction that Putin
does not want to be our friend.

00:28:55.240 --> 00:28:58.888
I think there's been so much effort
made by successive western governments,

00:28:58.888 --> 00:29:01.554
to have friendly and
cooperative relations with Putin.

00:29:01.554 --> 00:29:06.370
What is just they fail to understand
is that this is not Putin's objective.

00:29:06.370 --> 00:29:09.260
Vladimir Putin is a very,
don't get me wrong, a very strong leader.

00:29:09.260 --> 00:29:12.960
Actually, a very impressive
individual in many ways.

00:29:12.960 --> 00:29:14.580
I don't mean to demean him.

00:29:14.580 --> 00:29:17.103
He's one of the more impressive
leaders I've dealt with.

00:29:17.103 --> 00:29:20.982
But Vladimir Putin carries
an enormous chip on his shoulder for

00:29:20.982 --> 00:29:22.820
the fall of the Soviet Union.

00:29:22.820 --> 00:29:27.300
He believes that the west was
trying to destroy his country.

00:29:27.300 --> 00:29:31.070
And a large part of his
actions are designed simply to

00:29:31.070 --> 00:29:33.630
undermine the west as an end in itself.

00:29:33.630 --> 00:29:35.370
And that's kind of what he's made of.

00:29:37.262 --> 00:29:38.570
&gt;&gt; That's a good warning to keep in mind.

00:29:40.490 --> 00:29:43.150
I'd like to shift a little
bit now to leadership style.

00:29:44.460 --> 00:29:47.640
As you rose to prominence
in Canadian politics,

00:29:47.640 --> 00:29:52.450
you showed that you weren't afraid to
take the gloves off in political scraps.

00:29:52.450 --> 00:29:58.480
A style that some observers
called decidedly un-Canadian.

00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:01.265
What led you to adopt
this particular approach?

00:30:01.265 --> 00:30:03.459
&gt;&gt; I wanted to win.

00:30:03.459 --> 00:30:08.517
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I remember another conservative leader

00:30:08.517 --> 00:30:14.085
in a particular province saying to
me before a campaign, he was saying,

00:30:14.085 --> 00:30:18.951
Prime Minister, I respect you,
but I have a different style.

00:30:18.951 --> 00:30:20.503
I'm not going to kind of mix it up,

00:30:20.503 --> 00:30:22.840
I'm not going to go
negative on the opposition.

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:25.520
I'm going to take the high road,
and I said great.

00:30:25.520 --> 00:30:27.080
I said there's a word for that.

00:30:27.080 --> 00:30:27.680
He said what is it?

00:30:27.680 --> 00:30:30.520
Called a losing campaign.

00:30:30.520 --> 00:30:31.930
Look, a campaign's a campaign.

00:30:31.930 --> 00:30:34.920
They use the word campaign for a reason.

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:36.281
It's a combat.

00:30:36.281 --> 00:30:41.134
It doesn't mean you should
do anything to win.

00:30:41.134 --> 00:30:44.218
But you are making a contrast
with your opponents, and

00:30:44.218 --> 00:30:46.640
you have to be combative
from time to time.

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:49.513
And every successful leader is.

00:30:49.513 --> 00:30:52.910
You talk about all the great
statesmen of history.

00:30:52.910 --> 00:30:55.838
I guarantee if you go back and
look at their political records,

00:30:55.838 --> 00:30:57.506
they were successful combatants.

00:30:57.506 --> 00:31:02.345
Now, if you're a conservative, the liberal
media says this is terrible and

00:31:02.345 --> 00:31:03.790
unstatesman-like.

00:31:03.790 --> 00:31:05.890
But the other side does it all the time.

00:31:06.950 --> 00:31:09.560
So you just gotta do it,
you just gotta face that criticism.

00:31:09.560 --> 00:31:11.310
And don't be unfair.

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:15.920
I used to say, I've delivered some very
tough criticisms of my opponents, but

00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:20.860
I always had one criteria with our staff,
which was, is the criticism true?

00:31:22.240 --> 00:31:24.360
Is the criticism true?

00:31:24.360 --> 00:31:26.260
Don't level a criticism that's not true.

00:31:26.260 --> 00:31:27.550
That's going to back fire on you.

00:31:27.550 --> 00:31:30.100
But if you see a weakness, deliver it.

00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:34.995
Obviously you have to deliver
your own message as well.

00:31:34.995 --> 00:31:37.490
But that's a big part of it.

00:31:37.490 --> 00:31:38.850
I think the other part of it is,

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:42.400
I like to think the other part of my
success was not just winning elections.

00:31:42.400 --> 00:31:45.780
But ultimately, once you get there,
you have to know why you're there and

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:46.650
what it is you're trying to do.

00:31:47.940 --> 00:31:51.240
&gt;&gt; And when it comes to winning,
there's winning in the short term and

00:31:51.240 --> 00:31:54.610
then there's winning in the long term,
or medium to long term.

00:31:54.610 --> 00:31:57.680
You once said about your former
finance minister Jim Flaherty, and

00:31:57.680 --> 00:31:58.810
I'm going to quote.

00:31:58.810 --> 00:32:03.510
As fiercely partisan as he was,
Jim was also genuinely liked and

00:32:03.510 --> 00:32:05.410
respected by his opponents.

00:32:05.410 --> 00:32:08.880
That's something in this business,
something I envy.

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:10.931
I can't even get my friends to like me.

00:32:10.931 --> 00:32:15.980
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Humor aside, how important

00:32:15.980 --> 00:32:21.630
do you feel being liked is to securing
your victories and cementing your legacy?

00:32:24.090 --> 00:32:27.060
&gt;&gt; Well, look, in spite of my joke,

00:32:27.060 --> 00:32:32.420
I actually think I was widely liked by
the people who voted for me in fact.

00:32:32.420 --> 00:32:37.410
But look, being,
I'm kind of one of those guys.

00:32:37.410 --> 00:32:38.090
I think being,

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:42.169
I'm amazed how many people go into
politics because they want to be loved.

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:46.651
It's not really a good occupation
if you want to be loved.

00:32:46.651 --> 00:32:52.936
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I do think there are people who go into

00:32:52.936 --> 00:32:56.778
politics because they like the cameras and
microphones, but they can't sing or dance.

00:32:56.778 --> 00:33:01.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So look,

00:33:01.480 --> 00:33:03.660
I was not attracted to camera and
microphones.

00:33:03.660 --> 00:33:06.520
I was a bit made up a little bit
differently than most politicians but

00:33:06.520 --> 00:33:08.740
I think it's important to be respected.

00:33:08.740 --> 00:33:12.060
I think it's important to be
seen as having integrity.

00:33:12.060 --> 00:33:17.870
But being loved or being liked,
I think that's secondary.

00:33:17.870 --> 00:33:20.470
I think it's obviously
important that you treat,

00:33:20.470 --> 00:33:22.630
it's important even that you
treat opponents with respect.

00:33:22.630 --> 00:33:25.245
You can still treat them with
respect while attacking them, right?

00:33:25.245 --> 00:33:30.136
There's a difference between attacking
someone on a weakness that's

00:33:30.136 --> 00:33:34.220
legitimate than belittling them and
we try and avoid that.

00:33:34.220 --> 00:33:39.542
I think the key is to be respected,
especially in democratic politics and

00:33:39.542 --> 00:33:43.870
as I say, I don't know anybody who got
into politics to be liked and succeeded.

00:33:45.270 --> 00:33:50.130
&gt;&gt; So there's a question that falls
from this, and it's one that we discuss

00:33:50.130 --> 00:33:54.840
a lot at business school about what
tact you can take as a leader.

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:58.890
And it's, do you think that we as leaders
have to make a choice between being

00:33:58.890 --> 00:34:03.480
warm and
assertive in order to get the job done?

00:34:04.840 --> 00:34:08.880
&gt;&gt; Well look, I would say that leadership
in the political arena is a bit different

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:12.335
than leadership in business,
in the democratic political arena.

00:34:12.335 --> 00:34:14.618
In the Democratic political arena,

00:34:14.618 --> 00:34:19.780
there are different leadership styles
that are successful for different people.

00:34:19.780 --> 00:34:22.630
Not all leaders are the same,
no all approaches are the same,

00:34:22.630 --> 00:34:24.620
certainly not in politics.

00:34:24.620 --> 00:34:32.480
But I would say that the difference in
politics is no matter how good you are,

00:34:32.480 --> 00:34:35.930
you are going to have a tremendous
amount of opposition and criticism.

00:34:35.930 --> 00:34:38.150
We encourage that, right?

00:34:38.150 --> 00:34:42.695
I mean, this is the difference between
democratic politics and politics in

00:34:42.695 --> 00:34:48.240
non-democratic countries, is we keep our
leaders under scrutiny and criticism.

00:34:48.240 --> 00:34:52.960
In fact, in the parliamentary system,
unlike your system,

00:34:52.960 --> 00:34:59.420
the Prime Minister who wields most of
the power is not the head of state.

00:34:59.420 --> 00:35:02.290
I'm not, when I traveled abroad
the Governor General of Canada

00:35:02.290 --> 00:35:04.790
is first in diplomatic ranking,
not the Prime Minister.

00:35:04.790 --> 00:35:09.150
He is the one who carries the prestige
of office in the country.

00:35:09.150 --> 00:35:13.780
I'm the Prime Minister, yeah I had most
of the head of government power, but

00:35:13.780 --> 00:35:17.610
I had to be subjected daily to
attacks in the House of Commons.

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:22.850
We offset the power we grant somebody by
the degree of scrutiny we put them under.

00:35:22.850 --> 00:35:25.980
Your system's a little bit different,
but there's still commonality.

00:35:25.980 --> 00:35:28.390
And that's what we do.

00:35:29.530 --> 00:35:34.500
We don't have cults of personality
in democratic countries.

00:35:34.500 --> 00:35:38.790
That is one of the hallmarks
of an undemocratic society.

00:35:38.790 --> 00:35:44.540
When a leader is beyond criticism, when
a leader embodies the state, when a leader

00:35:44.540 --> 00:35:50.760
becomes somebody that you actually have
to worship, for lack of a better term.

00:35:50.760 --> 00:35:56.580
And so no matter what style
you adopt in public life,

00:35:56.580 --> 00:35:57.950
you're going to have lots of criticism.

00:35:57.950 --> 00:35:59.520
There's no way around that.

00:35:59.520 --> 00:36:02.570
Look, I think in business
there are ways of

00:36:02.570 --> 00:36:07.290
conducting yourself that would not expose
yourself to that level of criticism.

00:36:07.290 --> 00:36:13.850
&gt;&gt; Sure, one thing that maybe is more
common perhaps between politics and

00:36:13.850 --> 00:36:19.470
business is the need to build and
utilize power over time.

00:36:19.470 --> 00:36:22.532
And we actually have a course here at
Stanford at the Business School called

00:36:22.532 --> 00:36:26.100
Paths to Power, which is all about
how individuals build, retain,

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:28.360
lose power in organizations.

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:32.009
It seems based on your career that you
can teach the course without any notes.

00:36:32.009 --> 00:36:38.716
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What

00:36:38.716 --> 00:36:45.480
are the key lessons that you've learned
about amassing and retaining power?

00:36:45.480 --> 00:36:50.200
&gt;&gt; Look, I'm not sure there's any one,
I say there's any one path.

00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:55.520
I think if I look at the successes that
I had I would attribute them to two or

00:36:55.520 --> 00:36:56.540
three things.

00:36:56.540 --> 00:36:59.620
One of them as I said earlier
was the luck of timing.

00:37:01.060 --> 00:37:04.530
And a couple of times,
when I became leader of my party and

00:37:04.530 --> 00:37:08.740
then when I merged the parties,
these things happened

00:37:09.770 --> 00:37:13.770
because they happened at
particular point of time.

00:37:13.770 --> 00:37:17.480
And I was the right person,
position to do it.

00:37:17.480 --> 00:37:18.480
I didn't plan that.

00:37:18.480 --> 00:37:19.620
I couldn't have planned it.

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:23.490
I know that there were people
actually think I planned all of this.

00:37:23.490 --> 00:37:24.942
It couldn't have been planned, and so

00:37:24.942 --> 00:37:27.670
I just happened to be the right person
at the right time to do those things.

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:29.810
And that's life, right?

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:33.930
A lot of success in life is luck.

00:37:33.930 --> 00:37:36.400
There's no way around that,
starting with where you're born and

00:37:36.400 --> 00:37:40.440
when, all those things.

00:37:40.440 --> 00:37:45.712
I think one of the strengths I had that
served me well was that I had, and

00:37:45.712 --> 00:37:51.910
people understood me knew that I had
a particular view of public policy and

00:37:51.910 --> 00:37:55.910
a vision for where the country should go,
a direction for the country.

00:37:55.910 --> 00:38:01.070
And to the extent they shared that
direction, they could get behind it.

00:38:01.070 --> 00:38:04.780
Now, it didn't mean that I
didn't consult regularly.

00:38:04.780 --> 00:38:08.630
And frankly, I would say that although I
had a clear direction for the country,

00:38:08.630 --> 00:38:13.480
I have often said this, that very
seldom did I take a policy decision in

00:38:13.480 --> 00:38:18.720
government that was not overwhelmingly
supported by my party, very seldom.

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:25.580
I didn't abuse,
I didn't abuse the power that I had.

00:38:25.580 --> 00:38:27.660
But I did provide a sense of direction.

00:38:27.660 --> 00:38:30.480
Other people who lead on different basis.

00:38:30.480 --> 00:38:35.350
Some people lead, one of my predecessors
Brian Mulroney, I would say that he led on

00:38:35.350 --> 00:38:40.100
the basis of his ability to establish
warm relationships with colleagues.

00:38:40.100 --> 00:38:41.340
And he was a master at that.

00:38:42.430 --> 00:38:43.850
That was probably his strength.

00:38:43.850 --> 00:38:49.310
Pierre Trudeau, the father of
the current Prime Minister was a really

00:38:49.310 --> 00:38:55.370
philosophically driven Prime MInister and
led on a couple of key issues of the day.

00:38:55.370 --> 00:38:59.940
So different people have different styles,
mine served me well.

00:38:59.940 --> 00:39:03.420
I like to think I didn't
abuse it in this sense.

00:39:03.420 --> 00:39:05.640
I could have wielded a lot more power.

00:39:05.640 --> 00:39:08.910
I think I could probably still easily
be leader of my party if I wanted to.

00:39:08.910 --> 00:39:12.390
I mean,
I'm de facto the founder of my party.

00:39:12.390 --> 00:39:16.800
And I could have turned the party into,
essentially,

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:19.440
a personal political
vehicle if I'd wanted.

00:39:19.440 --> 00:39:21.010
But that was not my goal.

00:39:21.010 --> 00:39:26.330
My goal in political life,
I'm driven by my political conservatism.

00:39:26.330 --> 00:39:29.739
My goal in life was not just
to win an election and govern.

00:39:29.739 --> 00:39:34.335
My goal was to establish a long-term
conservative institutional force that

00:39:34.335 --> 00:39:38.340
would be a long-term contender for
power in government.

00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:41.980
And so I was determined to establish
an institutional organization that would

00:39:41.980 --> 00:39:46.240
outlive me and
would not need me down the road.

00:39:46.240 --> 00:39:51.880
So I did things very different than if I
simply wanted to amass power at all costs.

00:39:51.880 --> 00:39:56.310
&gt;&gt; And in your quest to establish that
long term conservative institutional

00:39:56.310 --> 00:40:01.260
platform, When the luck ran out, and
you were just left with decisions to make,

00:40:01.260 --> 00:40:04.910
you probably had to make some
choices that felt impossible.

00:40:04.910 --> 00:40:08.490
Did you ever make any decisions that
you wish you had made differently?

00:40:08.490 --> 00:40:09.690
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:40:09.690 --> 00:40:10.995
But I never say what those were.

00:40:10.995 --> 00:40:14.358
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, look,

00:40:14.358 --> 00:40:16.264
I would say a couple of things.

00:40:16.264 --> 00:40:19.399
Obviously, there are all kinds of
things you would do different.

00:40:21.720 --> 00:40:26.020
More important in a public policy sense,
did I make decisions

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:33.230
that I didn't want to make at the time.

00:40:33.230 --> 00:40:34.560
That's the more important thing,

00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:37.310
that I really wasn't
comfortable with at the time.

00:40:37.310 --> 00:40:39.232
The answer is yes.

00:40:39.232 --> 00:40:44.612
As a leader of a party, as somebody
dealing with political reality from time

00:40:44.612 --> 00:40:50.100
to time ahead to make decisions that
would not have been my first choice.

00:40:50.100 --> 00:40:54.100
That said,
I don't think on any really big thing

00:40:54.100 --> 00:40:57.610
that I ever find myself making a decision
that was fundamentally uncomfortable with.

00:40:57.610 --> 00:40:59.870
It may not even my optimal decision, but

00:40:59.870 --> 00:41:03.090
if I really thought it was
the wrong decision, a bad decision,

00:41:03.090 --> 00:41:10.190
as opposed to a non-optimal decision,
I would be pretty reluctant to take it.

00:41:10.190 --> 00:41:13.851
&gt;&gt; One last question before we shift
to questions from the audience.

00:41:13.851 --> 00:41:16.789
Another former finance minister,
Joe Oliver,

00:41:16.789 --> 00:41:21.430
said that one thing the media did not
portray enough i n the press, that is.

00:41:21.430 --> 00:41:22.108
&gt;&gt; Just one thing?

00:41:22.108 --> 00:41:23.517
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, [LAUGH],

00:41:23.517 --> 00:41:28.410
one of the things he felt, anyways,
was your passion and pride for Canada.

00:41:28.410 --> 00:41:32.484
Could you talk a little bit more
about where this passion comes from?

00:41:32.484 --> 00:41:36.610
&gt;&gt; I don't know where it comes from.

00:41:36.610 --> 00:41:40.312
To some degree,
I think it's the way it should be.

00:41:40.312 --> 00:41:41.753
I'm a seventh generation Canadian.

00:41:41.753 --> 00:41:45.011
I tell this story, my great, great, great,

00:41:45.011 --> 00:41:49.443
great grandfather was settled
in Sackville, New Brunswick,

00:41:49.443 --> 00:41:55.378
then part of Nova Scotia in 1774, the year
before the American Revolution, and

00:41:55.378 --> 00:42:01.000
was the local leader in the militia for
the crown against the revolutionaries.

00:42:01.000 --> 00:42:03.163
So it goes back a long way.

00:42:03.163 --> 00:42:06.860
But look, I'm a product of my country.

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:13.251
So much of what I've enjoyed in
life is because of my country.

00:42:13.251 --> 00:42:16.544
I don't think there are many
countries in the world where

00:42:16.544 --> 00:42:19.836
someone like me from a modest
middle class background and

00:42:19.836 --> 00:42:24.787
public school education would become the
leader of their country, a major country.

00:42:24.787 --> 00:42:28.360
I think that says a lot
about the kind of social and

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:32.380
economic opportunity and
mobility we have in Canada.

00:42:32.380 --> 00:42:35.600
I love our country's history,
I love our country's geography.

00:42:35.600 --> 00:42:39.200
I won't as far as to tell you
that I always love the weather.

00:42:39.200 --> 00:42:43.448
But I am always turning the temperature
down in hotel rooms when

00:42:43.448 --> 00:42:45.240
I'm outside of Canada.

00:42:45.240 --> 00:42:47.045
So I must like it somewhat.

00:42:47.045 --> 00:42:51.700
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But I think this is partly natural.

00:42:52.970 --> 00:42:56.320
What I don't understand, and
I say this about the other side,

00:42:56.320 --> 00:43:00.350
I don't understand the modern,
I would call modern elite liberalism,

00:43:00.350 --> 00:43:03.780
that often seems not to like its country.

00:43:03.780 --> 00:43:09.467
To me, being a nationalist, I'm not
talking a nativist or a xenophobe, but

00:43:09.467 --> 00:43:15.607
being a nationalist is something I would
have expected from any leader, most are.

00:43:15.607 --> 00:43:20.699
If you don't love your country, if you're
not deeply rooted in it, don't love it's

00:43:20.699 --> 00:43:26.290
people, don't love most things about it,
why are you leading it in the first place?

00:43:26.290 --> 00:43:30.867
So to me, this is just what you would
expect of someone who aspires to be head

00:43:30.867 --> 00:43:32.620
of government of a country.

00:43:33.940 --> 00:43:34.440
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:43:35.490 --> 00:43:39.562
Now we'll open it up to
questions from the audience.

00:43:39.562 --> 00:43:40.160
We've got one right there.

00:43:43.290 --> 00:43:44.037
&gt;&gt; Thanks very much for being here.

00:43:44.037 --> 00:43:47.636
My name is Audrey,
an undergrad in math and computer science.

00:43:47.636 --> 00:43:53.037
I was curious to hear your thoughts
on May 2008 financial crisis and

00:43:53.037 --> 00:43:58.360
broadly how you approached making
decisions and this whole process.

00:43:59.780 --> 00:44:03.830
&gt;&gt; Well, look obviously that
was an interesting time for

00:44:03.830 --> 00:44:10.085
me because I had begun my political career
and a policy since my number one focus,

00:44:10.085 --> 00:44:14.350
as economists, an economist whose
specialist is fiscal policy,

00:44:14.350 --> 00:44:19.240
was back in the 80s and
90s fixing Canada's fiscal problems.

00:44:19.240 --> 00:44:26.080
And creating a long term structural
balance, eliminating the debt problem.

00:44:26.080 --> 00:44:29.620
Of course, we did that in the 90s and
my first few years in government,

00:44:29.620 --> 00:44:34.060
I inherited a surplus, continued to run
a surplus, used it predominantly to reduce

00:44:34.060 --> 00:44:37.370
taxes by the way, which I think is
what a good conservative would do.

00:44:37.370 --> 00:44:40.911
But I didn’t use it to reverse taxes
in a way that would create a structural

00:44:40.911 --> 00:44:43.724
deficit, I wouldn't cut taxes
if I couldn't pay for it.

00:44:43.724 --> 00:44:48.400
All of a sudden we have
the 2008 financial crisis.

00:44:48.400 --> 00:44:54.640
People forget I'd even pledged in
the election campaign that I would not run

00:44:54.640 --> 00:44:59.000
a deficit,
despite what was impending recession.

00:44:59.000 --> 00:45:00.830
But what people forget is,

00:45:00.830 --> 00:45:05.610
each week, it was really something
to be in power at that time.

00:45:05.610 --> 00:45:10.480
From September of 2008 till, I would say,

00:45:10.480 --> 00:45:15.650
January, February of 2009,
we literally were in a situation where

00:45:15.650 --> 00:45:20.600
economic activity was visibly falling
around the globe every single week.

00:45:20.600 --> 00:45:22.000
And it was just incredible.

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:24.590
You'd wake up every morning

00:45:24.590 --> 00:45:28.050
with cataclysmic economic
news all around the world.

00:45:28.050 --> 00:45:32.605
Well, it became apparent to me and
it was fortunate I had the right training.

00:45:32.605 --> 00:45:35.216
I'm not just a fiscal policy economist,

00:45:35.216 --> 00:45:39.330
my background is in economic history and
macroeconomic theory.

00:45:39.330 --> 00:45:43.981
And it became very apparent to me
that we run what I thought was

00:45:43.981 --> 00:45:48.500
a kind of a theoretical situation,
but coming to real life.

00:45:48.500 --> 00:45:51.240
And I concluded it was necessary for
us to run

00:45:51.240 --> 00:45:55.360
not just allow the budget to fall into
deficit, but it became necessary to

00:45:55.360 --> 00:45:59.770
run a significant stimulus program because
of the collapse of economic activity.

00:45:59.770 --> 00:46:01.090
And so we did that.

00:46:01.090 --> 00:46:05.164
But we also, with Minister Flaherty's,
the late Jim Flaherty,

00:46:05.164 --> 00:46:07.214
Finance Minister's guidance.

00:46:07.214 --> 00:46:11.533
As soon as we did that, we put in place
a program to gradually restore economic

00:46:11.533 --> 00:46:14.170
balance, which we did over five years.

00:46:14.170 --> 00:46:15.180
So it was a tough decision.

00:46:15.180 --> 00:46:19.570
By the way,
not an easy one to get through my caucus.

00:46:19.570 --> 00:46:22.670
A large percentage of my caucus
did not want us to run a deficit,

00:46:22.670 --> 00:46:24.810
did not want to run the stimulus program.

00:46:24.810 --> 00:46:29.855
But I think I was able to convince them,
partly because I was so

00:46:29.855 --> 00:46:34.580
known as a fiscal hawk, and also because
of my training, I was able to convince

00:46:34.580 --> 00:46:38.370
them that we were in a situation that
required extraordinary measures.

00:46:38.370 --> 00:46:44.063
But I assured them and assured Canadians
that we would not ruin the long-term

00:46:44.063 --> 00:46:49.682
financial structure of the country,
and we ultimately succeeded in that.

00:46:51.346 --> 00:46:53.230
&gt;&gt; Ernesto Silva, here.

00:46:53.230 --> 00:46:55.452
I'm a member of the Chilean Congress.

00:46:55.452 --> 00:46:57.480
&gt;&gt; Could you please stand up?

00:46:57.480 --> 00:46:58.300
&gt;&gt; Please, I'm sorry.

00:46:59.610 --> 00:47:02.740
Ernesto Silva,
I'm a member of the Chilean Congress.

00:47:02.740 --> 00:47:05.580
You mentioned that you were writing a book

00:47:05.580 --> 00:47:09.323
about how conservatives should
face the rise of populism.

00:47:09.323 --> 00:47:11.117
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Could you comment a little bit on

00:47:11.117 --> 00:47:11.858
that, please.

00:47:11.858 --> 00:47:13.390
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'll do it very quick.

00:47:14.660 --> 00:47:22.570
First of all, it's interesting to hear the
term populist described today as it is.

00:47:22.570 --> 00:47:26.911
Usually, pejoratively and
often pejoratively by conservatives,

00:47:26.911 --> 00:47:30.218
populists are anti-market,
anti-trade, etc.

00:47:30.218 --> 00:47:33.968
The Reform Party that I was involved
in founding described itself as

00:47:33.968 --> 00:47:35.035
a populist party.

00:47:35.035 --> 00:47:38.410
[LAUGH] It was for trade and for markets.

00:47:38.410 --> 00:47:42.200
It was really a populous party in the then
tradition of Thatcher and Reagan.

00:47:42.200 --> 00:47:48.600
So populism is a term that kind of has
different meanings and different contexts.

00:47:48.600 --> 00:47:53.880
I often think that when you see the term
populist used in the media today,

00:47:53.880 --> 00:47:58.430
it's used this way, we describe
outcomes we like as democracy and

00:47:58.430 --> 00:48:00.125
outcomes we don't like as populism.

00:48:00.125 --> 00:48:03.870
So I think there's a bit
of a loaded term there.

00:48:03.870 --> 00:48:09.060
But to the extent that in
the globalization age, that I like to

00:48:09.060 --> 00:48:14.460
think we as Conservatives brought about,
globalization, freer trade, free markets.

00:48:14.460 --> 00:48:18.700
Freer migration around the world,
we're now seeing a backlash to that.

00:48:18.700 --> 00:48:24.210
And I really believe that Conservatives,
that is,

00:48:24.210 --> 00:48:29.370
people who are students of human
experience, rather than just decry that.

00:48:29.370 --> 00:48:33.960
When we see our own voters being attracted
to that we need to ask ourselves why.

00:48:33.960 --> 00:48:37.380
And I think in the age of globalization
that way too many ordinary people

00:48:37.380 --> 00:48:38.210
are not doing well enough.

00:48:39.310 --> 00:48:41.900
And when you look at some of
the things the populous complain

00:48:41.900 --> 00:48:43.320
about in terms of trade policy or

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:47.950
market policy or immigration policy,
globalism is a philosophy.

00:48:47.950 --> 00:48:49.836
I do think that in some cases,

00:48:49.836 --> 00:48:53.840
while we're generally on the right
track we pursue policies.

00:48:53.840 --> 00:48:58.501
That haven't really thought hard about
the actual impacts on ordinary people,

00:48:58.501 --> 00:49:00.360
that's what I always try to do.

00:49:00.360 --> 00:49:04.630
I would say I was not a blueprint kind of
politician, I don't believe in them.

00:49:04.630 --> 00:49:09.297
But I think in a lot of cases, politicians
have followed blueprints and have not been

00:49:09.297 --> 00:49:13.790
very conscious of how some market-oriented
policies actually impact people.

00:49:13.790 --> 00:49:17.140
And we should be making sure we
understand what those impacts are.

00:49:17.140 --> 00:49:22.522
Just to give an example,
in many Western countries,

00:49:22.522 --> 00:49:28.155
the large scale importation
of low-skilled workers.

00:49:28.155 --> 00:49:31.024
At a time when low-skilled workers
are under technological pressure and

00:49:31.024 --> 00:49:32.260
their wages are falling.

00:49:32.260 --> 00:49:36.684
This makes no sense in terms of
the social outcomes it produces.

00:49:36.684 --> 00:49:42.046
Allowing foreign non-residents
to buy significant blocks

00:49:42.046 --> 00:49:49.330
of residential real estate and leave
it unoccupied in major urban centers.

00:49:49.330 --> 00:49:53.420
Yeah, that's an open market policy,
but what is the social utility of that

00:49:53.420 --> 00:49:55.890
other than to drive people out
of the areas they live in?

00:49:57.110 --> 00:50:02.260
So I think we really have to think hard,
make sure, I'm a strong believer

00:50:02.260 --> 00:50:07.140
that over time, only market-oriented
policies really create growth.

00:50:07.140 --> 00:50:10.960
But they can take a lot of forms and

00:50:10.960 --> 00:50:13.750
you have to pursue them in ways
that are getting good outcomes.

00:50:13.750 --> 00:50:18.492
Too many working middle class people in
Western countries, especially this one,

00:50:18.492 --> 00:50:21.349
by the way,
have simply not been doing very well.

00:50:21.349 --> 00:50:27.793
And we have to understand why that is and

00:50:27.793 --> 00:50:32.096
adapt to those concerns.

00:50:32.096 --> 00:50:35.701
If voters go a different way,
no point telling them they're wrong,

00:50:35.701 --> 00:50:38.010
in a democracy the voters
are always right.

00:50:38.010 --> 00:50:41.574
So our voters are telling us, we're off
track, we better listen to what those

00:50:41.574 --> 00:50:45.365
messages are and figure out which messages
are right and which messages are wrong.

00:50:47.829 --> 00:50:48.912
Take one more question?

00:50:48.912 --> 00:50:54.138
&gt;&gt; Hi, my name is Amy, I'm a finance PhD
student here at the GSP, I'm also a proud

00:50:54.138 --> 00:50:59.370
Canadian who has been in the United States
since college, almost a decade ago.

00:51:00.440 --> 00:51:04.780
Over this period of time I sense a growing
divide between the coastal elite and

00:51:04.780 --> 00:51:06.170
the rest of America.

00:51:06.170 --> 00:51:11.810
As you travel around Canada, do you
sense the same trend of polarization?

00:51:11.810 --> 00:51:13.120
&gt;&gt; The answer to that is no.

00:51:14.700 --> 00:51:19.122
Canada has not had the kind
of political polarization or

00:51:19.122 --> 00:51:23.458
this modern manifestation
of more extreme populism.

00:51:23.458 --> 00:51:26.480
That we've seen in the United States and
other parts of the world.

00:51:26.480 --> 00:51:29.981
And the reason for that,
I like to think is really rather simple.

00:51:29.981 --> 00:51:35.369
It's that in Canada, middle and
working class people have had fairly

00:51:35.369 --> 00:51:41.170
steady income growth even through
the global financial crisis.

00:51:41.170 --> 00:51:48.040
And so I think that I think there may be
other factors driving the polarization.

00:51:48.040 --> 00:51:52.110
But Canada is not immune,
if we practise a series of

00:51:52.110 --> 00:51:55.250
bad economic policies over
a long enough period of time.

00:51:55.250 --> 00:51:56.927
And people start to feel dispossessed or

00:51:56.927 --> 00:51:59.851
ignored by the political system we
could get the same thing in Canada.

00:51:59.851 --> 00:52:03.664
But we don't have it today,
we have had political change,

00:52:03.664 --> 00:52:08.140
we have political debate,
we have parties with different views.

00:52:08.140 --> 00:52:11.640
But it's not a deeply
rooted social cleavage,

00:52:11.640 --> 00:52:13.770
which is what really strikes me here.

00:52:13.770 --> 00:52:18.440
Really strikes me about the United States
is not simply the political polarization.

00:52:18.440 --> 00:52:22.899
But the degree to which it actually
reflects, in my experience,

00:52:22.899 --> 00:52:24.448
the American public.

00:52:24.448 --> 00:52:27.038
I'll just tell you, I've told
Canadians for a long period of time,

00:52:27.038 --> 00:52:27.760
in my experience.

00:52:29.050 --> 00:52:31.750
George W Bush was president,
Barack Obama was president,

00:52:31.750 --> 00:52:33.880
obviously now with Donald Trump.

00:52:33.880 --> 00:52:38.082
I would say to people, if you go to
the United States don't give your

00:52:38.082 --> 00:52:41.638
opinion of George W Bush or
Barack Obama or Donald Trump.

00:52:41.638 --> 00:52:45.968
Until you know what the listener thinks
because I guarantee he's on one side or

00:52:45.968 --> 00:52:49.130
the other, and
that's just the way the country is here.

00:52:49.130 --> 00:52:51.818
And I think it's worrisome, but

00:52:51.818 --> 00:52:57.391
I think underlying it is the fact
that tens of millions of Americans.

00:52:57.391 --> 00:53:01.203
Who used to have a good middle
class lifestyle no longer do and

00:53:01.203 --> 00:53:04.359
this is a serious problem
that has got to be fixed.

00:53:05.430 --> 00:53:07.980
&gt;&gt; So
I wanted to wrap with a last question and

00:53:07.980 --> 00:53:10.330
draw on a few things that you
just talked about with us today.

00:53:10.330 --> 00:53:15.170
You've mentioned some pretty technocratic
terms, social utility and productivity,

00:53:15.170 --> 00:53:19.490
these are the terms of somebody who's
clearly deeply versed in economic policy.

00:53:19.490 --> 00:53:26.000
I wanted to step back though and ask
about the emotional aspect of leadership.

00:53:26.000 --> 00:53:27.320
And specifically,

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:32.120
the ways in which you unite people through
the emotional appeal of your leadership.

00:53:33.280 --> 00:53:37.960
And a lot of these themes that you've
spoken about with conservatism are still

00:53:37.960 --> 00:53:41.110
getting at what ultimately what the voters
ultimately want and what's best for them.

00:53:41.110 --> 00:53:46.640
How do you ensure that they connect
emotionally to your policy views and

00:53:46.640 --> 00:53:49.740
to ultimately what
conservatism is trying to do?

00:53:49.740 --> 00:53:52.310
&gt;&gt; Well look, it's a good question and
I'm not going to get up and

00:53:52.310 --> 00:53:56.830
claim it was one of my strengths, it's not
a strength of conservatism, by the way.

00:53:56.830 --> 00:54:00.560
I think if you look at other movements,

00:54:00.560 --> 00:54:05.205
whether they be on the right or
on the left, modern liberalism, socialism,

00:54:05.205 --> 00:54:09.545
nationalism, modern right-wing populism.

00:54:09.545 --> 00:54:15.389
These are at their heart,
more essentially emotional appeals,

00:54:15.389 --> 00:54:21.230
I do think, not that we have to
connect to people where they live,

00:54:21.230 --> 00:54:23.799
and they live emotionally.

00:54:23.799 --> 00:54:27.189
But I honestly think that people tend,
and I say to conservative leaders.

00:54:27.189 --> 00:54:32.004
People tend to turn to conservatives when
they're really deeply worried and they

00:54:32.004 --> 00:54:36.970
want sensible solutions, and when they
want something beyond the mere emotional.

00:54:36.970 --> 00:54:39.899
They want to be convinced that there's
somebody who actually knows what they're

00:54:39.899 --> 00:54:41.670
doing and that it will work.

00:54:41.670 --> 00:54:45.130
And so, while I think it's important
to connect emotionally, and

00:54:45.130 --> 00:54:47.800
we often don't do a good
enough job with that.

00:54:47.800 --> 00:54:51.696
I think conservatism can never be a purely
emotional political philosophy the way

00:54:51.696 --> 00:54:52.834
some of the others are.

00:54:54.220 --> 00:54:55.060
&gt;&gt; Ladies and gentlemen,

00:54:55.060 --> 00:54:56.660
please put your hands together for Mr

00:54:56.660 --> 00:55:01.736
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much!

00:55:01.736 --> 00:55:02.489
&gt;&gt; Thank you!

